 ## Overview
-The Brick ID Page Table Layout is a sparse memory management technique that maps virtual volume addresses to physical brick locations using a page table structure [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently accessing large, sparse volumetric datasets by decoupling the logical coordinate space from the physical storage layout [S1, unofficial].
+The Brick ID Page Table Layout is a sparse memory management technique that maps virtual volume addresses to physical brick locations using a page table structure [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently accessing large, sparse volumetric datasets by decoupling the logical coordinate space from the physical storage layout [S1, unofficial]. It differs from sibling methods like [ExaBricks Hierarchy](2182-exabricks-hierarchy.md), [Sparse Page Table and Brick Pool](2550-sparse-page-table-and-brick-pool.md), and [Compressed Segmentation Volume](4985-compressed-segmentation-volume.md) by using a flat, fixed-size 32-bit brick ID encoding for direct page table indexing rather than hierarchical tree traversal or complex compression schemes [S2].
 
